abstract={The Western Indo-Pacific damselfish, Dascyllus aruanus, is a highly site-attached fish living in permanent social groups associated with branching corals (Acropora spp., Pocillopora spp.) in which it shelters when threatened. In a mutualistic association with branching corals, this species provides beneficial services including increases in coral growth rates, provision of nutrients, removal of sediment, protection from predators, modulation of hydrodynamic conditions, subsidies of nitrogen and phosphorus or increases in colony (Chase et al. 2022). At least one species, Mulloidichthys flavolineatus, appeared to communicate, through colour pattern change, showing a potential willingness to be cleaned. [...]we suggest that D. aruanus is a facultative cleaner fish on coral reefs, providing a new example of mutualistic interaction between coral reef fishes.},
